<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 91 0 R
/T (A routing algorithm can be explained to children as a set of instructions or rules that help us decide the best way to get from one place to another. Just like when they play a game and have to find the quickest way to reach the finish line, a routing algorithm does something similar. It looks at all the possible paths, considers different factors like distance, time, and obstacles, and then chooses the best path to reach the destination. In the world of computers, a routing algorithm is like a very smart map. It helps information travel through the Internet, which is a bit like a big city filled with roads. Sometimes, there's a lot of traffic or road work, so the routing algorithm helps the information find the best path. It thinks about things like how long it will take and how many other computers the information needs to visit along the way. And it updates itself if anything changes.)
/E (A routing algorithm can be explained to children as a set of instructions or rules that help us decide the best way to get from one place to another. Just like when they play a game and have to find the quickest way to reach the finish line, a routing algorithm does something similar. It looks at all the possible paths, considers different factors like distance, time, and obstacles, and then chooses the best path to reach the destination. In the world of computers, a routing algorithm is like a very smart map. It helps information travel through the Internet, which is a bit like a big city filled with roads. Sometimes, there's a lot of traffic or road work, so the routing algorithm helps the information find the best path. It thinks about things like how long it will take and how many other computers the information needs to visit along the way. And it updates itself if anything changes.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
