298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (Orientation, at its core, is the ability to locate oneself in one's environment and navigate through it. Throughout human history, it has been a fundamental concept impacting our lives, routines, systems, and businesses. From ancient sailors using the stars to guide their way across the oceans, to modern-day drivers using GPS systems to navigate crowded city streets, orientation plays a crucial role in our everyday actions and decisions.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
299 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (In terms of routines and systems, orientation helps us create efficient paths for our daily commutes. On a larger scale, it influences urban planning, transportation networks, and international trade routes. This concept, although often taken for granted, greatly shapes our physical world and how we interact with it.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (The concept of routing algorithms can be seen as an exemplification of orientation. These algorithms, used in digital mapping tools and navigation systems, take various factors into account such as distance, time, and obstacles to determine the most efficient path from one location to another. In essence, they perform the same function that our brains do when we mentally map out our route to a destination.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
301 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (Building upon the "Pass the Parcel" activity where students acted as network nodes making routing decisions, this activity expands that understanding into real-world navigation. Just as students learned to make simple routing choices when passing balls through their human network, they will now apply similar decision-making processes i.e. heuristics to transportation routes. Students will design and analyze routes based on different transportation systems and constraints, developing a deeper understanding of how routing algorithms work in practice. Through this connection between the abstract concept of network routing and real-world navigation, they will improve their geographical understanding, problem-solving skills, and grasp of algorithmic thinking.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
