/E (During a first rapid roundtable, ask students to think about the different means of transport that exist and in which context they are used, by whom and how. You can do it with a simple roundtable or based on voluntary participation.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
311 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 645 0 R
/E (Then, divide the students into small groups, to research the different modes of transport identified more deeply: their efficiency, their costs, who can use them and how, what are their level of accessibility, and their environmental impact. They should identify if they are used for daily activities or if they are more punctual, and how this impacts the environmental footprint of the transport choice. This phase aims to build knowledge on getting around for daily travels.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
312 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 646 0 R
/E (Each group presents its findings to the class. Optionnaly, you can create an artistic commitment to the activity by asking each group to create a panel presenting the transport mode they have studied, which you can then hang in the classroom.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
313 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (Just like we experienced in the schoolyard with our human network, a map also represents a network of possible paths. In our activity, each student was a "node" that had to make simple decisions to move the balls \(our "data"\) toward their destination. Similarly, on a map, each intersection is a decision point where we must choose the best path. This approach helps us understand how navigation algorithms work in real life, whether we are finding our way on foot, by bicycle, by car, or by public transport.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
314 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (Divide again the classroom into small groups \(you can reuse the same groups as in the step before\). Each group is attributed with a transport system basically: pedestrian / bicycle / personal car / public bus. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5 6]
>>
endobj
315 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (Distribute a set of maps to all groups: pupils will work with two different scenarios. First, "the easy way" uses a basic street map with minimal obstacles. Provide then "the challenging way" by adding complexity information. You can use pictograms under your map to provide additional information to consider such as: traffic congestion, construction work, slippery paths or transport-specific limitations.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
316 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (Each group will create a routing algorithm for their assigned transport mode \(walking, cycling, driving, or taking the bus\). Your algorithm should find the best path from point A to point B while considering multiple factors.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
317 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 647 0 R
