<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 97 0 R
/E (As an additional stage, and if conditions allow it \(logistically, in terms of time or supervision\), an engaging conclusion to the lesson would be to plan an expedition that allows students to apply routing algorithms outside the classroom in real life. Choose a destination for the route. It should be a place that is relevant to the pupils and offers a variety of possible routes and intersections.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
398 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 97 0 R
/E (A relevant example for your pupils could be to choose the route from their current primary school to the secondary school most of them will attend the following years. This not only applies their learning in a real-world context but also helps them prepare for a significant upcoming change in their lives.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
399 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 97 0 R
/E (To test different decision-making choices, you can create groups and provide each of them with a navigation constraints: one can have a compass, another one a GPS and the last one can have the constraint to only follow street signs.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
400 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 670 0 R
/E (Compass constraints and interest: Using a compass in this activity presents an interesting challenge. Unlike modern digital mapping tools, a compass does not provide a pre-determined route or indicate the direction of the destination. Instead, the compass points to the North, requiring students to orient themselves based on their understanding of geographical directions and their location relative to the destination. The compass will guide them in choosing between two routes options. However, this choice may not always be straightforward. They might find that the direction chosen based on the compass leads to a closed road or a one-way street for instance that they will enlarge the travel. These real-world constraints will require them to constantly reassess and refine their choices, providing a deeper understanding of routing and the complexities of real-world navigation.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
