409 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 671 0 R
/E (GPS constraints and interest: Using GPS navigation provides students with precise location data and turn-by-turn directions. This tool offers real-time positioning and multiple route options. However, the challenge lies in understanding how the GPS calculates these routes and what factors it considers. Students will need to analyze why the GPS suggests certain paths over others, considering factors like traffic, distance, and road types. This helps them understand how digital routing algorithms work in practice and how they balance different variables to determine optimal routes.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
410 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 672 0 R
/E (Street signs constraints and interest: Following only street signs presents a unique navigation challenge that mirrors historical wayfinding methods. Students must rely on physical infrastructure and urban planning to reach their destination. This approach requires careful attention to street names, direction signs, and local landmarks. It demonstrates how cities are designed to be navigable without digital assistance and helps students develop spatial awareness. The limitation of using only street signs may lead to longer routes or temporary confusion, highlighting the evolution of navigation methods and the importance of clear urban signage.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
