591 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 91 0 R
/T (Explain how players must plan routes for their trains, highlighting the link with the concept of routing in the itinerary. Play one or two rounds to familiarize students with the idea. It is not mandatory to play the game until the end, but it will give a nice touch of a gamified vision of a complex concept to your pupils. Bring back the classroom in a normal setting, ask students to think about how they planned their routes in the game if they have played it, and how this might relate to programming routing algorithms in real life. Encourage them to think about the factors they had to consider in the game, such as distance and obstacles, and how they might apply to designing a routing algorithm.)
/E (Explain how players must plan routes for their trains, highlighting the link with the concept of routing in the itinerary. Play one or two rounds to familiarize students with the idea. It is not mandatory to play the game until the end, but it will give a nice touch of a gamified vision of a complex concept to your pupils. Bring back the classroom in a normal setting, ask students to think about how they planned their routes in the game if they have played it, and how this might relate to programming routing algorithms in real life. Encourage them to think about the factors they had to consider in the game, such as distance and obstacles, and how they might apply to designing a routing algorithm.)
/Pg 14 0 R
>>
endobj
